sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/turing-capable-network-topologies--grok-4-7--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Formalize equal-diffusion impossibility and the n=2 list" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/turing-capable-network-topologies> ;
    dct:description "Two theorems, both classical, both absent as checked lemmas. First, for every dimension n, if the diffusion matrix is a scalar multiple of the identity, every eigenvalue of J minus q squared D is an eigenvalue of J shifted by a non-positive real, so Hurwitz stability of J implies stability at every wave number. Equal diffusion cannot create a Turing instability. This does not contradict Marcon et al., who found patterning by mechanisms other than that linear instability; the theorem's statement must say diffusion-driven linear instability, not patterning. Second, for n = 2, derive the necessary and sufficient sign patterns: the inhibitor diffuses faster, and the Jacobian signs are the activator-inhibitor pattern or the substrate-depletion pattern. Use the Routh–Hurwitz criteria on the quadratic characteristic polynomial. Hypotheses are the signs and which species diffuse." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "No-sorry theorems: equal diffusivities forbid a Turing instability in every dimension, and for two species the Turing-capable sign patterns are exactly the two classical ones." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: A concrete 2-by-2 sign pattern outside those two classes, with numerical rates, that is stable without diffusion and unstable at some wave number. That would falsify the classification." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the Jacobian, the diagonal diffusion matrix and the dispersion relation. 2. Prove the equal-diffusion theorem for general n. 3. Specialize to n = 2 and compute the Hurwitz inequalities. 4. Case-split the sign patterns and prove the two classical patterns are exactly the Turing-capable ones. 5. Audit axioms." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: linear-algebra" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-ddi> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-wavelength> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-morphogen-hypothesis> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.1952.0012> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.14022> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/turing-capable-network-topologies> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Classify the network topologies that admit diffusion-driven instability" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.1952.0012> dct:title "The chemical basis of morphogenesis" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Turing 1952, The chemical basis of morphogenesis" .
  <https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.14022> dct:title "High-throughput mathematical analysis identifies Turing networks for patterning with equally diffusing signals" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Marcon et al. 2016, patterning with equal diffusion, which this theorem does not claim to forbid" .
}

